@ Index:   Tide 47 data: USA: NOAA (10 ports)
@ Author:  Victor Valdes
@ Version: 0.3.0
@ Source:  https://github.com/valdesvj/tide47
@ Tested:  with TIDE47 in the C47 simulator (tests/test_tide47.py)
@ Input:   X = a port's number (1-10), or 0 for the region (called by TIDE47)
@ Output:  the port's name, time zone, datum, source, Z0 and the matrix HG of its harmonic constants
@
@ ---- What it is ----
@ TIDEUS is a data program of Tide 47: the harmonic constants of the 10 ports of the region USA: NOAA, for TIDE47
@ to predict their tides. The ports: Boston, MA; New York (Battery); Charleston, SC; Key West, FL; Galveston, TX;
@ San Diego, CA; San Francisco, CA; Seattle, WA; Honolulu, HI; San Juan, PR.
@
@ ---- How to use it ----
@ Load it with TIDE47 and press XEQ TIDE47 (not XEQ TIDEUS: TIDE47 calls it). Load only the regions you want:
@ TIDE47 finds the data programs on the calculator, and + goes from one region to the next. Load the TIDEUS built
@ together with your TIDE47: an older one is left out with OLD DATA, REBUILD.
@
@ ---- What it changes on your calculator ----
@ Called by TIDE47 with a port's number, it sets the port's name, time zone, datum, source and mean level in
@ TIDE47's registers and writes the matrix HG (one number a constituent); TIDE47 deletes HG at its end. Nothing
@ else.
@
@ ---- Programs and labels ----
@ One program with one global label (TIDEUS); each port is a local label (LBL 01-10), LBL 99 the region.
@

LBL "TIDEUS"                    ; X = 0: the region (GTO 99); else the port X (XEQ IND)
X=0?
GTO 99
STO 99
XEQ IND 99
RTN
LBL 99                          ; the region: its title (R97), its number of ports (R96), the constituents (R93)
"USA: NOAA"
STO 97
10
STO 96
3060
STO 93
RTN
